Compile a comprehensive, organized database (catalogue) of the various adjective-qualified classes of modular forms that have appeared in the mathematical literature. For each class, the database should specify:

(a) a precise definition of the class (including the relevant group, weight, multiplier system, growth/transformation conditions, and any auxiliary data such as harmonic, holomorphic, or non-holomorphic structure);
(b) the transformation law under the relevant modular or congruence subgroup;
(c) representative examples;
(d) relationships and inclusions between this class and other classes in the catalogue.

The catalogue must include, at a minimum, entries for the following adjective-qualified notions of modular form: almost modular forms, magnetic modular forms, quasi-modular forms, and weakly modular forms (i.e., weakly holomorphic modular forms), and should be extended to encompass other named variants (such as mock modular, harmonic Maass, mixed mock modular, partial, false, holomorphic, meromorphic, nearly holomorphic, Jacobi, Hilbert, Siegel, Hecke, period, polar, quantum, and higher-depth quasi/mock variants, among others) where applicable.

Goal: produce such a database, with consistent definitions and cross-references, suitable as a reference for the theory of modular forms.

