An n×n Sudoku square (for n = k² a perfect square) is an n×n array filled with the integers 1, 2, …, n such that:
(i) each row contains every integer in {1, …, n} exactly once;
(ii) each column contains every integer in {1, …, n} exactly once;
(iii) when the array is partitioned into k×k subblocks (with k = √n), each of the n subblocks contains every integer in {1, …, n} exactly once.

Let S(n) denote the number of distinct valid Sudoku squares of order n. It is known that S(4) = 288 and S(9) = 6,670,903,752,021,072,936,960.

Problem: Find a closed-form expression (or general formula) for S(n) valid for all n = k², k ≥ 1.

