Consider a Darwinian individual-based branching particle system on a trait space $E$ (a Polish space) defined as follows. At any time $t\ge 0$ the population is described by a finite point measure $X^N_t = \tfrac{1}{N}\sum_{i\in I_t}\delta_{x_i(t)}$ on $E$, where $N$ is a scaling parameter and each particle $i$ carries a trait $x_i(t)\in E$. Each particle independently:

(i) moves in $E$ according to a Markov process with generator $A$ (e.g. a diffusion on $E$);

(ii) gives birth at rate $b(x)$ to a new particle whose trait is the parent's trait perturbed by a mutation kernel $m(x,dy)$;

(iii) dies at rate $d(x) + \tfrac{1}{N}\int_E c(x,y)\,X^N_t(dy)$, where $c(x,y)\ge 0$ models logistic competition between traits.

Assume the usual moment and regularity conditions ensuring that, as $N\to\infty$, $X^N$ converges in distribution (on Skorokhod path space) to a measure-valued process $\nu = (\nu_t)_{t\ge 0}$ taking values in the space of finite Borel measures on $E$; $\nu$ is the nonlinear superprocess associated with the parameters $(A,b,d,m,c)$.

For a measurable subset $B\subseteq E$ (a "sub-population"), write $\nu_t(B)$ for the mass of $\nu_t$ on $B$, and define the extinction time of the sub-population $B$ as
$$\tau_B = \inf\{t\ge 0:\ \nu_t(B)=0\},$$
with $\tau_E$ denoting the global extinction time.

Problem. Determine, in terms of the parameters $(A,b,d,m,c)$ and the initial measure $\nu_0$:

(a) the survival probability $\mathbb{P}(\tau_E = \infty)$ and the extinction probability $\mathbb{P}(\tau_E < \infty)$ of the global population;

(b) the law (or sharp asymptotics, including tail behaviour as $t\to\infty$) of the extinction time $\tau_E$;

(c) the analogous extinction/survival probabilities and the distribution of $\tau_B$ for sub-populations $B\subseteq E$, including how these depend on $B$ and on the interaction between sub-populations induced by the competition kernel $c$.

