Work in the untyped lambda-calculus. Recall that the Böhm tree BT(M) of a lambda-term M is the (possibly infinite) labelled tree obtained by repeatedly head-reducing: if M reduces to a head normal form λx₁…xₙ. y M₁ … Mₖ, then the root of BT(M) is labelled λx₁…xₙ.y and its children are BT(M₁), …, BT(Mₖ); if M has no head normal form the root is labelled ⊥.

A closed lambda-term M is called a hereditary permutation if its Böhm tree BT(M) satisfies all of the following:
  (i) no node of BT(M) carries the label ⊥;
  (ii) every variable bound in BT(M) occurs exactly once in the tree;
  (iii) at each node λx₁…xₙ.y M₁ … Mₖ the head variable y is x₁ (the first abstracted variable of that node);
  (iv) for i = 2, …, n, the bound variable xᵢ occurs as the head variable of an immediate child of the node where it is abstracted (i.e. each non-head bound variable occurs exactly one level below its abstraction).

Problem. Devise a type assignment system T (with some collection of types) and exhibit a fixed type-shape σ such that, for every closed lambda-term M:
  M is a hereditary permutation if and only if M is assigned in T a type of shape σ.

That is, give a type-theoretic characterization in which the hereditary permutations are exactly the closed terms typable with the prescribed fixed shape.

