representations.normalized:
A Pure Type System (PTS) is specified by a triple (S, A, R), where S is a set of sorts, A ⊆ S × S is a set of axioms, and R ⊆ S × S × S is a set of rules. Terms are built from a countable set of variables, the sorts in S, application (M N), abstraction (λx:A. M), and dependent product (Πx:A. B). Contexts are finite sequences x₁:A₁, …, xₙ:Aₙ. The typing judgement Γ ⊢ M : A is defined by the standard PTS inference rules:

- (axiom) ⊢ s₁ : s₂ for each (s₁, s₂) ∈ A;
- (start) Γ ⊢ A : s ⟹ Γ, x:A ⊢ x : A;
- (weakening) Γ ⊢ M : B and Γ ⊢ A : s ⟹ Γ, x:A ⊢ M : B;
- (product) Γ ⊢ A : s₁ and Γ, x:A ⊢ B : s₂ ⟹ Γ ⊢ (Πx:A.B) : s₃, for (s₁, s₂, s₃) ∈ R;
- (application) Γ ⊢ M : (Πx:A.B) and Γ ⊢ N : A ⟹ Γ ⊢ M N : B[x := N];
- (abstraction) Γ, x:A ⊢ M : B and Γ ⊢ (Πx:A.B) : s ⟹ Γ ⊢ (λx:A. M) : (Πx:A.B);
- (conversion) Γ ⊢ M : A, Γ ⊢ B : s, and A =_β B ⟹ Γ ⊢ M : B.

The conversion rule may be split into a reduction rule (where A β-reduces to B) and an expansion rule (where B β-reduces to A). The Expansion Postponement conjecture states: for every PTS and every derivable judgement Γ ⊢ M : A, there exists a derivation of this judgement in which the expansion rule is applied at most once, and only as the final step of the derivation.

Determine whether Expansion Postponement holds for every Pure Type System.

