Let G=(V,E) be a finite simple graph. A k-expression for G is a finite algebraic expression built from the following four operations on vertex-labeled graphs whose vertex labels are integers in {1,2,...,k}:
(i) creation of a single vertex with label i (denoted i);
(ii) disjoint union of two labeled graphs (denoted ⊕);
(iii) renaming all vertices of label i to label j (denoted ρ_{i→j});
(iv) adding all edges between vertices of label i and vertices of label j, for i≠j (denoted η_{i,j}).
The graph defined by such an expression is obtained by forgetting the labels. The clique-width cw(G) of G is the smallest k such that G can be constructed by some k-expression.

The decision problem CLIQUE-WIDTH-k is: given a graph G as input, decide whether cw(G) ≤ k.

Question: For every fixed integer k ≥ 4, can CLIQUE-WIDTH-k be decided in time polynomial in |V(G)|? Equivalently, is there, for each fixed k, a polynomial-time algorithm that recognizes the class of graphs of clique-width at most k?

