In a scientific measurement setting, suppose one obtains observations of the form X_i = μ + b(θ) + ε_i, i = 1, …, n, where μ is an unknown quantity of interest, ε_i are independent random errors with mean 0 and (possibly large) variance σ², and b(θ) is an unknown systematic bias depending on unobserved nuisance parameters θ describing instrument, environmental, or methodological conditions. Call such a setting an instance of "uncomfortable science" when σ is comparable to or larger than the effect size of interest |μ|, so that random fluctuations dominate any single measurement and standard repetition-based averaging alone cannot reliably reveal b(θ).

Develop a general methodology — i.e., a class of experimental designs, statistical estimators, and diagnostic procedures — that:

(a) provably detects the presence of a nonzero systematic bias b(θ) when σ is large relative to |b(θ)| and the number of replications n is moderate;

(b) produces a consistent (or at least asymptotically unbiased) estimator of μ in the presence of such bias, together with honest confidence intervals whose coverage does not degrade as σ grows;

(c) gives sharp lower bounds (in a minimax or information-theoretic sense) on the resources (sample size, number of independent conditions θ, prior information) required to separate b(θ) from random noise.

Formulate precise sufficient conditions on the structure of b(θ) (e.g., smoothness, sparsity, or dependence on observable covariates) and on the experimental design under which (a)–(c) are achievable, and exhibit explicit procedures attaining the bounds.

