Let $H_{0,1},\dots,H_{0,k}$ be null hypotheses tested on a common (or overlapping) data source, producing p-values $P_1,\dots,P_k$ that are, in general, statistically dependent with an unknown or only partially known joint dependence structure. Recall that Fisher's combination statistic
$$T_F = -2\sum_{i=1}^{k} \log P_i$$
follows a chi-squared distribution with $2k$ degrees of freedom under the global null hypothesis $H_0=\bigcap_{i=1}^k H_{0,i}$ only when $P_1,\dots,P_k$ are mutually independent and uniformly distributed on $(0,1)$.

Develop a general, principled, and broadly applicable method for combining the dependent p-values $P_1,\dots,P_k$ into a single test of the global null $H_0$ that:

(i) controls the type-I error at a prescribed level $\alpha$ under arbitrary (or at least a wide class of) dependence structures among $P_1,\dots,P_k$;
(ii) does not require knowledge of the full joint distribution or covariance structure of the underlying test statistics;
(iii) attains, under realistic alternatives, statistical power comparable to that of Fisher's method in the independent case;
(iv) is computationally tractable for large $k$.

Provide such a combination rule together with a proof of its validity and a characterization of its asymptotic and finite-sample power properties, or demonstrate that no method can simultaneously satisfy (i)–(iv).

