Model the spatial part of the universe as a connected, complete Riemannian 3-manifold (M, g), interpreted as a comoving spatial section of a Friedmann–Lemaître–Robertson–Walker–type spacetime. Observational data constrain the sectional curvature of (M, g) to be close to zero on observable scales, but do not pin down its global topology.

Determine which of the following holds for the underlying 3-manifold M:
(a) M is so large (relative to the diameter of the observable region) that its global topology is unmeasurable from any observations confined to the observable universe;
(b) M is homeomorphic to the Poincaré dodecahedral space, i.e. the spherical 3-manifold S^3 / I*, where I* is the binary icosahedral group acting freely on S^3 by isometries;
(c) M is homeomorphic to some other closed or open 3-manifold, in which case identify it.

Additionally, estimate or bound the total volume (or appropriate notion of size) of M, given that the diameter of the observable region is approximately 93 billion light-years.

