In observations of the cosmic microwave background (CMB) radiation—the relic electromagnetic radiation emitted approximately 13.8 billion years ago—certain large-angle features, in particular the quadrupole (ℓ=2) and octupole (ℓ=3) multipole moments of the temperature anisotropy expanded in spherical harmonics on the celestial sphere, exhibit preferred axes that lie unexpectedly close to one another and appear to align with the ecliptic plane and the direction of motion of the Solar System (the so-called "axis of evil"). Under the standard cosmological model, the CMB temperature fluctuations should be statistically isotropic, so such alignments should occur with only small probability by chance.

Determine the origin of this apparent alignment. Specifically, decide which of the following explanations is correct:
(a) the alignment is an artifact of systematic errors in the data processing pipeline (e.g. foreground subtraction, masking, or map-making);
(b) the alignment is produced by contamination from local astrophysical effects (such as zodiacal emission, solar-system foregrounds, or local large-scale structure);
(c) the alignment reflects a genuine violation of the Copernican/cosmological principle of large-scale statistical isotropy, requiring new physics beyond the standard ΛCDM model.

Provide a rigorous statistical and physical argument distinguishing among (a), (b), and (c).

