Determine whether, during the early history of the Solar System, the proto-Earth underwent a collision with another planet-sized body (commonly referred to as Theia) whose aftermath produced the Moon. Specifically, decide whether such a giant-impact event actually occurred, and if so, characterize:

(a) the mass, composition, and impact geometry (velocity, angle, spin state) of the impactor required to be consistent with present-day observations;
(b) the resulting orbital evolution of the Earth–Moon system, including the initial Earth–Moon separation immediately after the impact and the subsequent tidal evolution leading to today's lunar orbit, accounting for the observed historical decrease in Earth's rotational period and in the lunar synodic month;
(c) the isotopic and geochemical constraints, in particular the near-identical oxygen, titanium, and tungsten isotope ratios of terrestrial and lunar samples, which must be reproduced by any successful impact scenario.

Provide either a definitive confirmation/refutation of the giant-impact hypothesis or identify the physical parameters of the collision that simultaneously satisfy all dynamical, geochemical, and isotopic constraints.

