In atmospheric dynamics, a storm track is a geographically preferred region (typically over the mid-latitude oceans) where extratropical cyclones form, intensify, and propagate, and where eddy kinetic energy and poleward heat and momentum fluxes are strongly concentrated. Storm tracks exhibit characteristic features such as zonal extent, seasonal modulation, downstream development, midwinter suppression (notably over the North Pacific), and sensitivity to the underlying jet stream, stationary waves, sea-surface temperature gradients, orography, diabatic heating, and moisture.

Problem: Identify and quantify the physical mechanisms that govern the dynamics of mid-latitude storm tracks. Specifically, determine a predictive theoretical framework that explains:
(a) the location, intensity, and seasonal cycle of the principal storm tracks;
(b) the relative contributions of baroclinic instability of the time-mean flow, nonlinear eddy–eddy interactions, diabatic processes (latent heating, radiation), surface boundary conditions (SST gradients, sea-ice, land–sea contrast), and orographic forcing;
(c) the observed midwinter suppression of the Pacific storm track despite maximum baroclinicity;
(d) the response of storm-track position and intensity to changes in external forcing such as greenhouse-gas-induced climate change.

Provide a closed dynamical theory (e.g., a reduced model, scaling laws, or energy/momentum budget) from which the above features can be derived rather than merely diagnosed.

