In the Haag–Kastler / algebraic framework of quantum field theory, a (bosonic) quantum field theory on 4-dimensional Minkowski spacetime $\mathbb{R}^{1,3}$ is specified by a net of unital $C^*$-algebras $\{\mathcal{A}(\mathcal{O})\}$ indexed by open bounded causally complete regions $\mathcal{O}\subset\mathbb{R}^{1,3}$, together with a continuous representation $\alpha:\mathcal{P}_+^{\uparrow}\to\mathrm{Aut}(\mathcal{A})$ of the proper orthochronous Poincaré group on the quasi-local algebra $\mathcal{A}=\overline{\bigcup_\mathcal{O}\mathcal{A}(\mathcal{O})}$, satisfying the Haag–Kastler axioms:
(i) isotony: $\mathcal{O}_1\subset\mathcal{O}_2\Rightarrow\mathcal{A}(\mathcal{O}_1)\subset\mathcal{A}(\mathcal{O}_2)$;
(ii) microcausality (locality): if $\mathcal{O}_1$ and $\mathcal{O}_2$ are spacelike separated then $[\mathcal{A}(\mathcal{O}_1),\mathcal{A}(\mathcal{O}_2)]=0$;
(iii) Poincaré covariance: $\alpha_g(\mathcal{A}(\mathcal{O}))=\mathcal{A}(g\mathcal{O})$ for all $g\in\mathcal{P}_+^{\uparrow}$;
(iv) existence of a vacuum state $\omega_0$ on $\mathcal{A}$ that is Poincaré invariant and whose GNS representation satisfies the spectrum condition (the generators of translations have joint spectrum contained in the closed forward light cone).

Call such a theory "interacting" if its $S$-matrix in the GNS representation of the vacuum is not the identity, equivalently if the theory is not unitarily equivalent to a free field theory.

