Hadwiger's conjecture states that for every positive integer t, every graph with chromatic number at least t contains the complete graph K_t as a minor (equivalently, every loopless graph G satisfies χ(G) ≤ η(G), where η(G) is the largest t such that G has a K_t minor). Write χ(H) for the chromatic number and ω(H) for the clique number of a graph H. For graphs F_1, …, F_k, a graph is (F_1, …, F_k)-free if it contains none of F_1, …, F_k as an induced subgraph. Let P_n denote the path on n vertices, C_n the cycle on n vertices, and P_5^c the complement of P_5. An ISK_4 in a graph is an induced subdivision of K_4; a graph is ISK_4-free if it contains no such induced subgraph.

Prove Hadwiger's conjecture for each of the following classes of graphs:
(1) graphs G such that every induced subgraph H of G satisfies χ(H) − ω(H) ≤ 2;
(2) (P_5, C_5)-free graphs;
(3) (P_5, P_5^c)-free graphs;
(4) ISK_4-free graphs.

Additionally, determine whether the class of (P_5, P_5^c)-free graphs satisfies a linear Hadwiger bound, i.e., whether there exists a constant c > 0 such that every (P_5, P_5^c)-free graph G satisfies χ(G) ≤ c · η(G).

