For an integer $k\geq 1$, let $\mathcal{G}_k(n)$ denote the family of all $n$-vertex simple graphs that do not contain $K_k$ as a minor (here $K_k$ is the complete graph on $k$ vertices; if one prefers the convention in which $C_k$ denotes a fixed graph parameter, replace $K_k$ by that graph throughout — the question is about minor-closed classes excluding a fixed graph of "size" $k$). A graph $H$ is said to be subgraph-universal for a family $\mathcal{F}$ of graphs if every graph in $\mathcal{F}$ is isomorphic to a subgraph of $H$.

Question: Does there exist a function $f:\mathbb{Z}_{\geq 1}\to \mathbb{Z}_{\geq 1}$ such that for every $k\geq 1$ there exists, for each $n$, a graph $H_{k,n}$ with the following properties:
(i) $H_{k,n}$ contains no $K_{f(k)}$-minor;
(ii) $|V(H_{k,n})| \leq n^{c(k)}$ for some constant $c(k)$ depending only on $k$;
(iii) every graph in $\mathcal{G}_k(n)$ is isomorphic to a subgraph of $H_{k,n}$?

