Let a, b, c be integers with a > 0. Consider the quadratic polynomial f(m) = a m^2 + b m + c, viewed as a function of a positive integer variable m. Assume the following necessary conditions hold:
(i) f is irreducible over the integers;
(ii) the leading coefficient a is positive;
(iii) there is no prime p that divides f(m) for every integer m (equivalently, gcd over all integers m of f(m) equals 1).
Under these assumptions, determine whether the set { m ∈ ℤ_{>0} : f(m) is prime } is infinite. In particular, decide whether for every such f there are infinitely many positive integers m for which a m^2 + b m + c is a prime number. As a notable special case, decide whether there are infinitely many primes of the form m^2 + 1.

