representations.normalized:
Let $(P,<)$ be a finite poset, and for each $x \in P$ let $G_x$ be a (partizan) combinatorial game in the sense of Conway, i.e., a game defined recursively by its sets of Left and Right options, with Left and Right alternating moves and the player unable to move losing (normal play convention).

Define the poset-indexed compound $G(P) = G((G_x)_{x\in P})$ to be the game played as follows. A position consists of the current positions of the component games $G_x$, together with the set $A \subseteq P$ of elements that are still "active." Initially $A = P$. On a turn, the player to move chooses an active element $x \in A$ that is minimal in $A$ with respect to the induced order from $P$, and makes a legal move (as Left or Right, respectively) in $G_x$ from its current position. If $G_x$ has no legal moves available to the moving player, that element is declared finished and removed from $A$ (alternatively, $x$ is removed from $A$ only once the component game $G_x$ has ended); play then continues on the remaining active components. A player who cannot move on any minimal active element loses.

This construction specializes to:
\begin{itemize}
\item the disjunctive sum $G_1 + G_2 + \cdots + G_n$ when $P$ is an antichain;
\item the sequential compound $G_1 \to G_2 \to \cdots \to G_n$ of Stromquist–Ullman when $P$ is a chain $x_1 < x_2 < \cdots < x_n$.
\end{itemize}

Develop a coherent theory of the compounds $G(P)$. In particular, establish:
\begin{enumerate}
\item a well-defined notion of game value for $G(P)$, including how the value depends functorially on $P$ and on the components $G_x$;
\item identities, inequalities, and simplification rules relating $G(P)$ to disjunctive sums and sequential compounds (recovering the known theories at the two extremes);
\item a theory of equivalence and dominance of options within $G(P)$, and conditions on $P$ and on $(G_x)$ under which the value of $G(P)$ can be computed from the values of the components $G_x$ alone;
\item the corresponding theory for impartial games, including formulas or algorithms for the Sprague–Grundy value of $G(P)$ in terms of $P$ and the Grundy values of $G_x$.
\end{enumerate}

