Let n ≥ 2 and let E^n denote n-dimensional Euclidean space. Suppose F is a family of subsets of E^n with the following closure properties:

(i) for every nonempty subfamily G ⊆ F, the intersection ⋂_{A ∈ G} A belongs to F;
(ii) for every A ∈ F and every v ∈ E^n, the translate A + v = { a + v : a ∈ A } belongs to F;
(iii) for every A ∈ F and every p ∈ E^n, the point reflection of A through p, namely { 2p − a : a ∈ A }, belongs to F.

Assume further that F contains at least one nonempty bounded open subset of E^n.

Must F necessarily contain a nondegenerate convex set (i.e. a convex subset of E^n with nonempty interior)?

