representations.normalized:
Let $\Omega \subset \mathbb{R}^n$ with $n \geq 3$ be a bounded domain whose boundary $\partial\Omega$ is smooth except at finitely many isolated singular points where it has cusps. A point $p \in \partial\Omega$ is called an outer (outward) cusp if, in suitable local coordinates near $p$, the domain $\Omega$ lies locally outside a region of the form $\{x_n \geq \varphi(|x'|)\}$ where $\varphi: [0,\varepsilon) \to [0,\infty)$ is a smooth increasing function with $\varphi(0)=\varphi'(0)=0$ and the complement contains an inward-tapering peak; it is called an inner (inward) cusp if $\Omega$ locally contains the corresponding outward-tapering peak. For the classical harmonic layer potentials on $\partial\Omega$, namely the single-layer operator
$$ (V\sigma)(x) = \int_{\partial\Omega} E(x-y)\sigma(y)\,dS(y), \qquad x \in \partial\Omega, $$
and the double-layer operator
$$ (W\mu)(x) = \int_{\partial\Omega} \frac{\partial E(x-y)}{\partial \nu_y}\mu(y)\,dS(y), \qquad x \in \partial\Omega, $$
where $E$ is the fundamental solution of the Laplacian in $\mathbb{R}^n$ and $\nu$ is the outward unit normal (defined away from the cusps), develop a full theory of these (and the associated direct and indirect) boundary integral equations for the interior and exterior Dirichlet and Neumann problems for the Laplace equation on $\Omega$ and on $\mathbb{R}^n \setminus \overline{\Omega}$. The theory should:
\begin{enumerate}
\item identify pairs of function spaces (e.g. weighted Sobolev, Besov, or trace spaces adapted to the cusp geometry, possibly described in terms of the cusp profile function $\varphi$) in which $V$ and $\tfrac{1}{2}I \pm W$ are bounded;
\item establish necessary and sufficient solvability conditions, Fredholm properties, indices, kernels, and cokernels of these operators in terms of the cusp profile;
\item characterize regularity and asymptotic behavior of solutions and densities near the cusp points;
\item treat both inner cusps (cusps pointing into $\Omega$) and outer cusps (cusps pointing into $\mathbb{R}^n \setminus \overline{\Omega}$) in dimension $n \geq 3$.
\end{enumerate}
Produce such a theory.

