representations.normalized:
Let a rigid body occupy a bounded domain in three-dimensional space that intersects the horizontal plane {x_3 = 0} (the mean free surface of an inviscid, incompressible fluid of infinite depth). Denote by S the wetted surface of the body (the part lying in the lower half-space {x_3 < 0}), by W the unbounded portion of {x_3 = 0} exterior to the body (the free surface), and by L = S ∩ {x_3 = 0} the water line. The body moves with constant velocity in the negative x_1-direction, and one seeks a velocity potential u defined in the fluid domain F = {x_3 < 0} \ S satisfying:
 (i) Δu = 0 in F;
 (ii) ∂u/∂n = f on S, where n is the unit normal to S and f is a prescribed datum (e.g. n · e_1);
 (iii) the linearized free-surface (Kelvin) condition ∂²u/∂x_1² + ν ∂u/∂x_3 = 0 on W, with ν > 0 a fixed parameter (ν = g/c² where c is the speed and g gravity);
 (iv) u → 0 as |x| → ∞ in F together with the standard radiation condition (no waves ahead of the body).
This is the Neumann–Kelvin problem for a surface-piercing body. It is known that, without additional data on L, the corresponding boundary integral operator is not Fredholm in natural function spaces.

Let L^+ ⊂ L denote the subset of points of the water line where the angle between the exterior unit normal ν_L to L (within the plane {x_3 = 0}) and the x_1-axis is at most π/2 (i.e. ν_L · e_1 ≥ 0).

Conjecture to verify: the Neumann–Kelvin problem (i)–(iv) becomes Fredholm in suitable function spaces if one additionally prescribes the values of ∂u/∂x_1 at every point of L^+ (interpreted as boundary traces from W, with existence of ∂u/∂x_1 near L assumed in the spirit of the two-dimensional asymptotics).

Prove or disprove this conjecture: establish (or refute) the Fredholm property of the augmented problem, and identify the function-space setting in which it holds.

