A permutation of length n is a bijection from {1,2,...,n} to itself. Given two permutations σ (of length k) and π (of length n), we say σ is contained as a pattern in π if there exist indices 1 ≤ i_1 < i_2 < ... < i_k ≤ n such that π(i_1), π(i_2), ..., π(i_k) are in the same relative order as σ(1), σ(2), ..., σ(k); otherwise π avoids σ. A permutation class C is a set of permutations that is closed under pattern containment: if π ∈ C and σ is contained in π as a pattern, then σ ∈ C. The generating function of C is the formal power series f_C(x) = Σ_{n ≥ 0} |C_n| x^n, where C_n is the set of permutations in C of length n.

Recall that a formal power series f(x) ∈ Q[[x]] is:
 • rational if there exist polynomials P, Q ∈ Q[x] with Q ≠ 0 such that f = P/Q;
 • algebraic if there exists a nonzero polynomial P(x,y) ∈ Q[x,y] such that P(x, f(x)) = 0;
 • D-finite (holonomic) if f satisfies a nontrivial linear differential equation with polynomial coefficients in x.

Problem. Provide necessary and sufficient conditions on a permutation class C that characterize exactly:
 (a) when f_C is rational;
 (b) when f_C is algebraic;
 (c) when f_C is D-finite;
and, more generally, for other comparable regularity classes of formal power series.

