Let $D$ be a bounded simply connected planar domain that is the union of unit squares of the square grid $\mathbb{Z}^2$ (a "domain" of the lattice), and let the boundary $\partial D$ be partitioned into two disjoint arcs $\partial_+$ and $\partial_-$, each a connected arc on the boundary of $D$, meeting at two distinguished boundary vertices $a$ and $b$. Fix a constant $\lambda \in (0, 1/2]$.

Consider the uniform probability measure on the (compact, convex) set of real-valued functions $h \colon V(D) \to \mathbb{R}$ on the vertices of $D$ satisfying:
\begin{itemize}
\item $h(v) = \lambda$ for every vertex $v \in \partial_+$;
\item $h(v) = -\lambda$ for every vertex $v \in \partial_-$;
\item $|h(v) - h(u)| \le 1$ for every edge $[u, v]$ of $D$.
\end{itemize}
From such a height function $h$ one can construct, in the standard way, an interface (lattice path) running from $a$ to $b$ separating vertices where $h \ge 0$ from those where $h < 0$.

Now consider a sequence of such discrete domains $(D_n, \partial_+^n, \partial_-^n)$ approximating, in the Carathéodory sense with respect to fixed interior points, a fixed simply connected planar domain $(\mathcal{D}, \partial_+, \partial_-)$ with two marked boundary prime ends, as the mesh size tends to zero.

Questions:
\begin{enumerate}
\item Does there exist a value $\lambda \in (0, 1/2]$ for which the law of the associated discrete interface converges, as the mesh tends to zero, to chordal $\mathrm{SLE}_4$ in $\mathcal{D}$ from one marked boundary point to the other?
\item For such a $\lambda$ (or otherwise), does the law of $h$, suitably interpreted (e.g. as a random distribution or via smooth test functions), converge in a natural sense to the Gaussian free field on $\mathcal{D}$ with boundary values $+\lambda$ on $\partial_+$ and $-\lambda$ on $\partial_-$ (up to a multiplicative constant)?
\end{enumerate}

