Let $\kappa \in (0,8)$, and let $\gamma : [0,\infty) \to \mathbb{C}$ denote the trace of a chordal Schramm–Loewner evolution $\mathrm{SLE}_\kappa$ in the upper half-plane (or any conformal image thereof in the plane). It is known that almost surely the Hausdorff dimension of $\gamma$ equals $d(\kappa) = 1 + \kappa/8$.

Construct a Borel measure $\mu$ supported on the trace $\gamma$ such that:
(i) $\mu$ is almost surely $\sigma$-finite;
(ii) $\mu$ is almost surely finite on every compact subset of $\mathbb{C}$;
(iii) $\mu$ is defined via a Hausdorff-type construction (i.e., it is obtained from a gauge function $\varphi$ through the corresponding Hausdorff measure $\mathcal{H}^\varphi$ restricted to the trace), and it captures the intrinsic size of the $\mathrm{SLE}_\kappa$ trace in a natural, conformally meaningful way.

Equivalently: identify an explicit gauge function $\varphi(r)$ such that the Hausdorff measure $\mathcal{H}^\varphi$ restricted to the $\mathrm{SLE}_\kappa$ trace is almost surely a non-trivial, locally finite, $\sigma$-finite measure.

