Let X be an infinite topological space. An open cover U of X is called:
• an ω-cover if X is not a member of U and every finite subset of X is contained in some member of U;
• a γ-cover if U is infinite and every point of X belongs to all but finitely many members of U;
• a τ-cover if U is a point-infinite open cover (each x ∈ X lies in infinitely many U ∈ U) such that for every two points x, y ∈ X, at least one of the sets {U ∈ U : x ∈ U and y ∉ U} or {U ∈ U : y ∈ U and x ∉ U} is finite.

Let Ω, Γ, T denote the families of open ω-covers, open γ-covers and open τ-covers of X, respectively. For families A, B of open covers, write (A choose B) for the property: every U ∈ A contains a subcover V ⊆ U with V ∈ B.

Question: Is the selection property (Ω choose Γ) — every open ω-cover of X contains a γ-subcover — equivalent (for all topological spaces X) to the selection property (Ω choose T) — every open ω-cover of X contains a τ-subcover?

