representations.normalized:
Let $X$ be a set of real numbers (or more generally a topological space). All covers below are by open subsets of $X$ and are assumed not to contain $X$ as an element.

A cover $\mathcal{U}$ of $X$ is called:
- an $\omega$-cover if every finite subset of $X$ is contained in some member of $\mathcal{U}$;
- a $\gamma$-cover if $\mathcal{U}$ is infinite and every point of $X$ belongs to all but finitely many members of $\mathcal{U}$;
- a $\tau$-cover if $\mathcal{U}$ is a countable $\omega$-cover such that for any two points $x, y \in X$, either $\{U \in \mathcal{U} : x \in U,\ y \notin U\}$ is finite, or $\{U \in \mathcal{U} : y \in U,\ x \notin U\}$ is finite.

Let $\mathcal{O}$, $\Omega$, $\Gamma$, $\mathrm{T}$ denote the collections of all open covers, $\omega$-covers, $\gamma$-covers, and $\tau$-covers of $X$, respectively.

For families $\mathcal{A}, \mathcal{B}$ of covers, define:
- $\mathsf{S}_1(\mathcal{A}, \mathcal{B})$: for every sequence $(\mathcal{U}_n)_{n \in \mathbb{N}}$ with $\mathcal{U}_n \in \mathcal{A}$, one can choose $U_n \in \mathcal{U}_n$ such that $\{U_n : n \in \mathbb{N}\} \in \mathcal{B}$;
- $\mathsf{U}_{\mathrm{fin}}(\mathcal{A}, \mathcal{B})$: for every sequence $(\mathcal{U}_n)_{n \in \mathbb{N}}$ with $\mathcal{U}_n \in \mathcal{A}$ (and no $\mathcal{U}_n$ containing a finite subcover of $X$), one can choose finite $\mathcal{F}_n \subseteq \mathcal{U}_n$ such that $\{\bigcup \mathcal{F}_n : n \in \mathbb{N}\} \in \mathcal{B}$.

Question: Does every set of reals satisfying $\mathsf{S}_1(\Omega, \mathrm{T})$ necessarily satisfy $\mathsf{U}_{\mathrm{fin}}(\mathcal{O}, \Gamma)$?

