representations.normalized:
For a topological space $X$, let $\mathcal{O}$ denote the collection of all open covers of $X$. An open cover $\mathcal{U}$ of $X$ (with $X \notin \mathcal{U}$) is called:
- a $\gamma$-cover ($\mathcal{U}\in\Gamma$) if $\mathcal{U}$ is infinite and every $x\in X$ belongs to all but finitely many members of $\mathcal{U}$;
- an $\omega$-cover ($\mathcal{U}\in\Omega$) if every finite subset of $X$ is contained in some member of $\mathcal{U}$;
- a $\tau$-cover ($\mathcal{U}\in T$) if it is a large $\omega$-cover (each $x\in X$ belongs to infinitely many members of $\mathcal{U}$) such that for any two $x,y\in X$, either $\{U\in\mathcal{U}: x\in U,\ y\notin U\}$ is finite or $\{U\in\mathcal{U}: y\in U,\ x\notin U\}$ is finite.

Given classes $\mathcal{A},\mathcal{B}$ of covers, define the selection principles:
- $\mathsf{S}_1(\mathcal{A},\mathcal{B})$: for every sequence $\mathcal{U}_n\in\mathcal{A}$ ($n\in\mathbb{N}$), one can pick $U_n\in\mathcal{U}_n$ with $\{U_n:n\in\mathbb{N}\}\in\mathcal{B}$;
- $\mathsf{S}_{\mathrm{fin}}(\mathcal{A},\mathcal{B})$: for every such sequence one can pick finite $\mathcal{F}_n\subseteq\mathcal{U}_n$ with $\bigcup_n\mathcal{F}_n\in\mathcal{B}$;
- $\mathsf{U}_{\mathrm{fin}}(\mathcal{A},\mathcal{B})$: for every sequence $\mathcal{U}_n\in\mathcal{A}$ of covers with no finite subcover, there exist finite $\mathcal{F}_n\subseteq\mathcal{U}_n$ such that $\{\bigcup\mathcal{F}_n:n\in\mathbb{N}\}\in\mathcal{B}$.

The Borel analogues are defined by restricting the covers to consist of Borel sets.

A property $\mathsf{P}$ of subsets of a fixed space (e.g. the reals) is called additive (under finite unions) if whenever $X$ and $Y$ both have property $\mathsf{P}$, so does $X\cup Y$.

Question: Is any of the properties
$$\mathsf{S}_1(T,T),\ \mathsf{S}_{\mathrm{fin}}(T,T),\ \mathsf{S}_1(\Gamma,T),\ \mathsf{S}_{\mathrm{fin}}(\Gamma,T),\ \mathsf{U}_{\mathrm{fin}}(\mathcal{O},T),$$
or any of their Borel analogues, additive under finite unions?

