Let $X$ be an infinite topological space (typically a Tychonoff space such as a set of reals). A cover $\mathcal{U}$ of $X$ by proper open subsets is called:
- a large cover ($\Lambda$) if every $x \in X$ belongs to infinitely many members of $\mathcal{U}$;
- an $\omega$-cover ($\Omega$) if every finite subset $F \subseteq X$ is contained in some member of $\mathcal{U}$;
- a $\tau$-cover ($T$) if it is a large cover and, for all $x, y \in X$, either $\{U \in \mathcal{U} : x \in U,\ y \notin U\}$ is finite or $\{U \in \mathcal{U} : y \in U,\ x \notin U\}$ is finite;
- a $\gamma$-cover ($\Gamma$) if it is infinite and every $x \in X$ belongs to all but finitely many members of $\mathcal{U}$.

For families $\mathcal{A}$ and $\mathcal{B}$ of open covers of $X$, the splittability property $\mathrm{Split}(\mathcal{A}, \mathcal{B})$ asserts: every cover $\mathcal{U} \in \mathcal{A}$ can be partitioned into two disjoint subfamilies $\mathcal{U}_0, \mathcal{U}_1$ such that each $\mathcal{U}_i$ contains a subfamily that is a member of $\mathcal{B}$ (equivalently, each $\mathcal{U}_i$ is a cover of type $\mathcal{B}$).

Questions:
(1) Does $\mathrm{Split}(\Omega, T)$ imply $\mathrm{Split}(T, \Gamma)$?
(2) If not, does $\mathrm{Split}(\Omega, T)$ at least imply $\mathrm{Split}(\Lambda, \Lambda)$?

