Let $\mathbb{R}$ denote the real line with its usual topology and Lebesgue measure, and let $2^{\mathbb{N}} = \{0,1\}^{\mathbb{N}}$ denote the Cantor space with the product topology and the standard product (coin-flipping) measure. For a Polish abelian group $(G,+)$ (such as $\mathbb{R}$ or $2^{\mathbb{N}}$, where addition in $2^{\mathbb{N}}$ is coordinatewise modulo $2$), a set $X\subseteq G$ is called:
\begin{itemize}
\item \emph{meager-additive} if for every meager set $M\subseteq G$, the algebraic sum $X+M=\{x+m:x\in X,\ m\in M\}$ is again meager;
\item \emph{null-additive} if for every Lebesgue-null (respectively, Haar-null) set $N\subseteq G$, the algebraic sum $X+N$ is null.
\end{itemize}
Identify the unit interval $[0,1]\subseteq\mathbb{R}$ with $2^{\mathbb{N}}$ via the standard binary expansion map (which is a bijection up to a countable set).

Question: Suppose $X\subseteq[0,1]$ is meager-additive (respectively, null-additive) when regarded as a subset of $\mathbb{R}$. Must $X$ then also be meager-additive (respectively, null-additive) when regarded, via the above identification, as a subset of the Cantor space $2^{\mathbb{N}}$?

